e\n125 |             about ethical issues in data science and machine learning.\n126 | \n127 |             In this special case, you can fetch the dataset from the original\n128 |             source::\n129 | \n130 |                 import pandas as pd\n131 |                 import numpy as np\n132 | \n133 |                 data_url = \"http://lib.stat.cmu.edu/datasets/boston\"\n134 |                 raw_df = pd.read_csv(data_url, sep=\"\\\\s+\", skiprows=22, header=None)\n135 |                 data = np.hstack([raw_df.values[::2, :], raw_df.values[1::2, :2]])\n136 |                 target = raw_df.values[1::2, 2]\n137 | \n138 |             Alternative datasets include the California housing dataset and the\n139 |             Ames housing dataset. You can load the datasets as follows::\n140 | \n141 |                 from sklearn.datasets import fetch_california_housing\n142 |                 housing = fetch_california_housing()\n143 | \n144 |             for the California housing dataset and::\n145 | \n146 |                 from sklearn.datasets import fetch_openml\n147 |                 housing = fetch_openml(name=\"house_prices\", as_frame=True)\n148 | \n149 |             for the Ames housing dataset.\n150 | \n151 |             [1] M Carlisle.\n152 |             \"Racist data destruction?\"\n153 |             <https://medium.com/@docintangible/racist-data-destruction-113e3eff54a8>\n154 | \n155 |             [2] Harrison Jr, David, and Daniel L. Rubinfeld.\n156 |             \"Hedonic housing prices and the demand for clean air.\"\n157 |             Journal of environmental economics and management 5.1 (1978): 81-102.\n158 |             <https://www.researchgate.net/publication/4974606_Hedonic_housing_prices_and_the_demand_for_clean_air>\n159 |             \"\"\"\n160 |         )\n161 |         raise ImportError(msg)\n162 |     try:\n163 |         return globals()[name]\n164 |     except KeyError:\n165 |         # This is turned into the appropriate ImportError\n166 |         raise AttributeError\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"get_tags\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `get_tags`.\n```\n293 | def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n294 |     \"\"\"Get estimator tags.\n295 | \n296 |     :class:`~sklearn.BaseEstimator` provides the estimator tags machinery.\n297 |     However, if an estimator does not inherit from this base class, we should\n298 |     fall-back to the default tags.\n299 | \n300 |     For scikit-learn built-in estimators, we should still rely on\n301 |     `self.__sklearn_tags__()`. `get_tags(est)` should be used when we\n302 |     are not sure where `est` comes from: typically\n303 |     `get_tags(self.estimator)` where `self` is a meta-estimator, or in\n304 |     the common checks.\n305 | \n306 |     .. versionadded:: 1.6\n307 | \n308 |     Parameters\n309 |     ----------\n310 |     estimator : estimator object\n311 |         The estimator from which to get the tag.\n312 | \n313 |     Returns\n314 |     -------\n315 |     tags : :class:`~.sklearn.utils.Tags`\n316 |         The estimator tags.\n317 |     \"\"\"\n318 | \n319 |     if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_tags__\"):\n320 |         tags = estimator.__sklearn_tags__()\n321 |     else:\n322 |         warnings.warn(\n323 |             f\"Estimator {estimator} has no __sklearn_tags__ attribute, which is \"\n324 |             \"defined in `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator`. This will raise an error in \"\n325 |             \"scikit-learn 1.8. Please define the __sklearn_tags__ method, or inherit \"\n326 |             \"from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` and other appropriate mixins such as \"\n327 |             \"`sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, `sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, \"\n328 |             \"`sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and `sklearn.base.ClusterMixin`, and \"\n329 |             \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`.\",\n330 |             category=FutureWarning,\n331 |         )\n332 |  