Mixin ABC for EAs to implement str methods\n 6 | \n 7 | Most methods on the StringMethods accessor follow the pattern:\n 8 | \n 9 |     1. extract the array from the series (or index)\n10 |     2. Call that array's implementation of the string method\n11 |     3. Wrap the result (in a Series, index, or DataFrame)\n12 | \n13 | Pandas extension arrays implementing string methods should inherit from\n14 | pandas.core.strings.base.BaseStringArrayMethods. This is an ABC defining\n15 | the various string methods. To avoid namespace clashes and pollution,\n16 | these are prefixed with `_str_`. So ``Series.str.upper()`` calls\n17 | ``Series.array._str_upper()``. The interface isn't currently public\n18 | to other string extension arrays.\n19 | \"\"\"\n20 | # Pandas current implementation is in ObjectStringArrayMixin. This is designed\n21 | # to work on object-dtype ndarrays.\n22 | #\n23 | # BaseStringArrayMethods\n24 | #  - ObjectStringArrayMixin\n25 | #     - StringArray\n26 | #     - NumpyExtensionArray\n27 | #     - Categorical\n28 | #     - ArrowStringArray\n29 | \n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n221 | def _validate(data):\n222 |         \"\"\"\n223 |         Auxiliary function for StringMethods, infers and checks dtype of data.\n224 | \n225 |         This is a \"first line of defence\" at the creation of the StringMethods-\n226 |         object, and just checks that the dtype is in the\n227 |         *union* of the allowed types over all string methods below; this\n228 |         restriction is then refined on a per-method basis using the decorator\n229 |         @forbid_nonstring_types (more info in the corresponding docstring).\n230 | \n231 |         This really should exclude all series/index with any non-string values,\n232 |         but that isn't practical for performance reasons until we have a str\n233 |         dtype (GH 9343 / 13877)\n234 | \n235 |         Parameters\n236 |         ----------\n237 |         data : The content of the Series\n238 | \n239 |         Returns\n240 |         -------\n241 |         dtype : inferred dtype of data\n242 |         \"\"\"\n243 |         if isinstance(data, ABCMultiIndex):\n244 |             raise AttributeError(\n245 |                 \"Can only use .str accessor with Index, not MultiIndex\"\n246 |             )\n247 | \n248 |         # see _libs/lib.pyx for list of inferred types\n249 |         allowed_types = [\"string\", \"empty\", \"bytes\", \"mixed\", \"mixed-integer\"]\n250 | \n251 |         data = extract_array(data)\n252 | \n253 |         values = getattr(data, \"categories\", data)  # categorical / normal\n254 | \n255 |         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n256 | \n257 |         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n258 |             raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n259 |         return inferred_dtype\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods.decode`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2068 | def decode(self, encoding, errors: str = \"strict\"):\n2069 |         \"\"\"\n2070 |         Decode character string in the Series/Index using indicated encoding.\n2071 | \n2072 |         Equivalent to :meth:`str.decode` in python2 and :meth:`bytes.decode` in\n2073 |         python3.\n2074 | \n2075 |         Parameters\n2076 |         ----------\n2077 |         encoding : str\n2078 |             Specifies the encoding to be used.\n2079 |         errors : str, optional\n2080 |             Specifies the error handling scheme.\n2081 |             Possible values are those supported by :meth:`bytes.decode`.\n2082 | \n2083 |         Returns\n2084 |         -------\n2085 |         Series or Index\n2086 |             A Series or Index with decoded strings.\n2087 | \n2088 |         See Also\n2089 |         --------\n2090 |         Series.str.encode : Encodes strings into bytes in a Series/Index.\n2091 | \n2092 |         Examples\n2093 |         --------\n2094 |         For Series:\n2095 | \n2096 |         >>> ser = pd.Series([b\"cow\", b\"123\", b\"()\"])\n2097 |         >>> ser.str.decode(\"ascii\")\n2098 |         0   cow\n2099 |         1   123\n2100 |         2   ()\n2101 |         dtype: object\n2102 |         \"\"\"\n2103 |         # TODO: Add a similar _bytes interface.\n2104 |         if encoding in _cpython_optimized_decoders:\n2105 |             # CPython optimized implementation\n2106 |             f = lambda x: x.decode(encoding, errors)\n2107 |         else:\n2108 |             decoder = codecs.getdecoder(encoding)\n2109 |             f = lambda x: decoder(x, errors)[0]\n2110 |         arr = self._data.array\n2111 |         # assert isinstance(arr, (StringArray,))\n2112 |         result = arr._str_map(f)\n2113 |         return self._wrap_result(result)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods.encode`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2116 | def encode(self, encoding, errors: str = \"strict\"):\n2117 |         \"\"\"\n2118 |         Encode character string in the Series/Index using indicated encoding.\n2119 | \n2120 |         Equivalent to :meth:`str.encode`.\n2121 | \n2122 |         Parameters\n2123 |         ----------\n2124 |         encoding : str\n2125 |             Specifies the encoding to be used.\n2126 |         errors : str, optional\n2127 |             Specifies the error handling scheme.\n2128 |             Possible values are those supported by :meth:`str.encode`.\n2129 | \n2130 |         Returns\n2131 |         -------\n2132 |         Series/Index of objects\n2133 |             A Series or Index with strings encoded into bytes.\n2134 | \n2135 |         See Also\n2136 |         --------\n2137 |         Series.str.decode : Decodes bytes into strings in a Series/Index.\n2138 | \n2139 |         Examples\n2140 |         --------\n2141 |         >>> ser = pd.Series([\"cow\", \"123\", \"()\"])\n2142 |         >>> ser.str.encode(encoding=\"ascii\")\n2143 |         0     b'cow'\n2144 |         1     b'123'\n2145 |         2      b'()'\n2146 |         dtype: object\n2147 |         \"\"\"\n2148 |         result = self._data.array._str_encode(encoding, errors)\n2149 |         return self._wrap_result(result, returns_string=False)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:cat_safe`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3743 | def cat_safe(list_of_columns: list[npt.NDArray[np.object_]], sep: str):\n3744 |     \"\"\"\n3745 |     Auxiliary function for :meth:`str.cat`.\n3746 | \n3747 |     Same signature as cat_core, but handles TypeErrors in concatenation, which\n3748 |     happen if the arrays in list_of columns have the wrong dtypes or content.\n3749 | \n3750 |     Parameters\n3751 |     ----------\n3752 |     list_of_columns : list of numpy arrays\n3753 |         List of arrays to be concatenated with sep;\n3754 |         these arrays may not contain NaNs!\n3755 |     sep : string\n3756 |         The separator string for concatenating the columns.\n3757 | \n3758 |     Returns\n3759 |     -------\n3760 |     nd.array\n3761 |         The concatenation of list_of_columns with sep.\n3762 |     \"\"\"\n3763 |     try:\n3764 |         result = cat_core(list_of_columns, sep)\n3765 |     except TypeError:\n3766 |         # if there are any non-string values (wrong dtype or hidden behind\n3767 |         # object dtype), np.sum will fail; catch and return with better message\n3768 |         for column in list_of_columns:\n3769 |             dtype = lib.infer_dtype(column, skipna=True)\n3770 |             if dtype not in [\"string\", \"empty\"]:\n3771 |                 raise TypeError(\n3772 |                     \"Concatenation requires list-likes containing only \"\n3773 |                     \"strings (or missing values). Offending values found in \"\n3774 |             