n13 | \n14 | Extracting colors from a continuous colormap\n15 | --------------------------------------------\n16 | \"\"\"\n17 | \n18 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n19 | import numpy as np\n20 | \n21 | import matplotlib as mpl\n22 | \n23 | n_lines = 21\n24 | cmap = mpl.colormaps['plasma']\n25 | \n26 | # Take colors at regular intervals spanning the colormap.\n27 | colors = cmap(np.linspace(0, 1, n_lines))\n28 | \n29 | fig, ax = plt.subplots(layout='constrained')\n30 | \n31 | for i, color in enumerate(colors):\n32 |     ax.plot([0, i], color=color)\n33 | \n34 | plt.show()\n35 | \n36 | # %%\n37 | #\n38 | # Extracting colors from a discrete colormap\n39 | # ------------------------------------------\n40 | # The list of all colors in a `.ListedColormap` is available as the ``colors``\n41 | # attribute.\n42 | \n43 | colors = mpl.colormaps['Dark2'].colors\n44 | \n45 | fig, ax = plt.subplots(layout='constrained')\n46 | \n47 | for i, color in enumerate(colors):\n48 |     ax.plot([0, i], color=color)\n49 | \n50 | plt.show()\n51 | \n52 | # %%\n53 | # See Also\n54 | # --------\n55 | #\n56 | # For more details about manipulating colormaps, see :ref:`colormap-manipulation`.  To\n57 | # change the default color cycle, see :ref:`color_cycle`.\n58 | #\n59 | # .. admonition:: References\n60 | #\n61 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n62 | #    in this example:\n63 | #\n64 | #    - `matplotlib.colors.Colormap`\n65 | #    - `matplotlib.colors.Colormap.resampled`\n66 | #\n67 | # .. tags::\n68 | #\n69 | #    component: colormap\n70 | #    styling: color\n71 | #    plot-type: line\n72 | #    level: intermediate\n73 | \n```\n\nFound class `lib/matplotlib/colorizer.py:Colorizer`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass Colorizer:\n    def __init__(self, cmap=None, norm=None):\n...\n    def _scale_norm(self, norm, vmin, vmax, A):\n...\n    @p