 = np.random.rand(N)\n17 | y = np.random.rand(N)\n18 | colors = np.random.rand(N)\n19 | area = (30 * np.random.rand(N))**2  # 0 to 15 point radii\n20 | \n21 | plt.scatter(x, y, s=area, c=colors, alpha=0.5)\n22 | plt.show()\n23 | \n24 | # %%\n25 | #\n26 | # .. admonition:: References\n27 | #\n28 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n29 | #    in this example:\n30 | #\n31 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n32 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/scatter_hist.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ============================\n  3 | Scatter plot with histograms\n  4 | ============================\n  5 | \n  6 | Show the marginal distributions of a scatter plot as histograms at the sides of\n  7 | the plot.\n  8 | \n  9 | For a nice alignment of the main Axes with the marginals, two options are shown\n 10 | below:\n 11 | \n 12 | .. contents::\n 13 |    :local:\n 14 | \n 15 | While `.Axes.inset_axes` may be a bit more complex, it allows correct handling\n 16 | of main Axes with a fixed aspect ratio.\n 17 | \n 18 | An alternative method to produce a similar figure using the ``axes_grid1``\n 19 | toolkit is shown in the :doc:`/gallery/axes_grid1/scatter_hist_locatable_axes`\n 20 | example.  Finally, it is also possible to position all Axes in absolute\n 21 | coordinates using `.Figure.add_axes` (not shown here).\n 22 | \n 23 | Let us first define a function that takes x and y data as input, as well\n 24 | as three Axes, the main Axes for the scatter, and two marginal Axes. It will\n 25 | then create the scatter and histograms inside the provided Axes.\n 26 | \"\"\"\n 27 | \n 28 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 29 | import numpy as np\n 30 | \n 31 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n 32 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n 33 | \n 34 | # some random data\n 35 | x = np.random.randn(1000)\n 36 | y 