*x*, *y*.\n1543 | \n1544 |         The optional parameter *fmt* is a convenient way for defining basic\n1545 |         formatting like color, marker and linestyle. It's a shortcut string\n1546 |         notation described in the *Notes* section below.\n1547 | \n1548 |         >>> plot(x, y)        # plot x and y using default line style and color\n1549 |         >>> plot(x, y, 'bo')  # plot x and y using blue circle markers\n1550 |         >>> plot(y)           # plot y using x as index array 0..N-1\n1551 |         >>> plot(y, 'r+')     # ditto, but with red plusses\n1552 | \n1553 |         You can use `.Line2D` properties as keyword arguments for more\n1554 |         control on the appearance. Line properties and *fmt* can be mixed.\n1555 |         The following two calls yield identical results:\n1556 | \n1557 |         >>> plot(x, y, 'go--', linewidth=2, markersize=12)\n1558 |         >>> plot(x, y, color='green', marker='o', linestyle='dashed',\n1559 |         ...      linewidth=2, markersize=12)\n1560 | \n1561 |         When conflicting with *fmt*, keyword arguments take precedence.\n1562 | \n1563 | \n1564 |         **Plotting labelled data**\n1565 | \n1566 |         There's a convenient way for plotting objects with labelled data (i.e.\n1567 |         data that can be accessed by index ``obj['y']``). Instead of giving\n1568 |         the data in *x* and *y*, you can provide the object in the *data*\n1569 |         parameter and just give the labels for *x* and *y*::\n1570 | \n1571 |         >>> plot('xlabel', 'ylabel', data=obj)\n1572 | \n1573 |         All indexable objects are supported. This could e.g. be a `dict`, a\n1574 |         `pandas.DataFrame` or a structured numpy array.\n1575 | \n1576 | \n1577 |         **Plotting multiple sets of data**\n1578 | \n1579 |         There are various ways to plot multiple sets of data.\n1580 | \n1581 |         - The most straight forward way is just to call `plot` multiple times.\n1582 |           Example:\n1583 | \n1584 |           >>> plot(x1, y1, 'bo')\n1585 |           >>> plot(x2, y2, 'go')\n1586 | \n1587 |         - If *x* and/or *y* are 2D arrays, a separate data set will be drawn\n1588 |           for every column. If both *x* and *y* are 2D, they must have the\n1589 |           same shape. If only one of them is 2D with shape (N, m) the other\n1590 |           must have length N and will be used for every data set m.\n1591 | \n1592 |           Example:\n1593 | \n1594 |           >>> x = [1, 2, 3]\n1595 |           >>> y = np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]])\n1596 |           >>> plot(x, y)\n1597 | \n1598 |           is equivalent to:\n1599 | \n1600 |           >>> for col in range(y.shape[1]):\n1601 |           ...     plot(x, y[:, col])\n1602 | \n1603 |         - The third way is to specify multiple sets of *[x]*, *y*, *[fmt]*\n1604 |           groups::\n1605 | \n1606 |           >>> plot(x1, y1, 'g^', x2, y2, 'g-')\n1607 | \n1608 |           In this case, any additional keyword argument applies to all\n1609 |           datasets. Also, this syntax cannot be combined with the *data*\n1610 |           parameter.\n1611 | \n1612 |         By default, each line is assigned a different style specified by a\n1613 |         'style cycle'. The *fmt* and line property parameters are only\n1614 |         necessary if you want explicit deviations from these defaults.\n1615 |         Alternatively, you can also change the style cycle using\n1616 |         :rc:`axes.prop_cycle`.\n1617 | \n1618 | \n1619 |         Parameters\n1620 |         ----------\n1621 |         x, y : array-like or scalar\n1622 |             The horizontal / vertical coordinates of the data points.\n1623 |             *x* values are optional and default to ``range(len(y))``.\n1624 | \n1625 |             Commonly, these parameters are 1D arrays.\n1626 | \n1627 |             They can also be scalars, or two-dimensional (in that case, the\n1628 |             columns represent separate data sets).\n1629 | \n1630 |             These arguments cannot be passed as keywords.\n1631 | \n1632 |         fmt : str, optional\n1633 |             A format string, e.g. 'ro' for red circles. See the *Notes*\n1634 |             section for a full description of the format strings.\n1635 | \n1636 |             Format strings are just an abbreviation for quickly setting\n1637 |             basic line properties. All of these and more can also be\n1638 |             controlled by keyword arguments.\n1639 | \n1640 |             This argument cannot be passed as keyword.\n1641 | \n1642 |         data : indexable object, optional\n1643 |             An object with labelled data. If given, provide the label names to\n1644 |             plot in *x* and *y*.\n1645 | \n1646 |             .. note::\n1647 |                 Technically there's a slight ambiguity in calls where the\n1648 |                 second label is a valid *fmt*. ``plot('n', 'o', data=obj)``\n1649 |                 could be ``plt(x, y)`` or ``plt(y, fmt)``. In such cases,\n1650 |                 the former interpretation is chosen, but a warning is issued.\n1651 |                 You may suppress the warning by adding an empty format string\n1652 |                 ``plot('n', 'o', '', data=obj)``.\n1653 | \n1654 |         Returns\n1655 |         -------\n1656 |         list of `.Line2D`\n1657 |             A list of lines representing the plotted data.\n1658 | \n1659 |         Other Parameters\n1660 |         ----------------\n1661 |         scalex, scaley : bool, default: True\n1662 |             These parameters determine if the view limits are adapted to the\n1663 |             data limits. The values are passed on to\n1664 |             `~.axes.Axes.autoscale_view`.\n1665 | \n1666 |         **kwargs : `~matplotlib.lines.Line2D` properties, optional\n1667 |             *kwargs* are used to specify properties like a line label (for\n1668 |             auto legends), linewidth, antialiasing, marker face color.\n1669 |             Example::\n1670 | \n1671 |             >>> plot([1, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3], 'go-', label='line 1', linewidth=2)\n1672 |             >>> plot([1, 2, 3], [1, 4, 9], 'rs', label='line 2')\n1673 | \n1674 |             If you specify multiple lines with one plot call, the kwargs apply\n1675 |             to all those lines. In case the label object is iterable, each\n1676 |             element is used as labels for each set of data.\n1677 | \n1678 |             Here is a list of available `.Line2D` properties:\n1679 | \n1680 |             %(Line2D:kwdoc)s\n1681 | \n1682 |         See Also\n1683 |         --------\n1684 |         scatter : XY scatter plot with markers of varying size and/or color (\n1685 |             sometimes also called bubble chart).\n1686 | \n1687 |         Notes\n1688 |         -----\n1689 |         **Format Strings**\n1690 | \n1691 |         A format string consists of a part for color, marker and line::\n1692 | \n1693 |             fmt = '[marker][line][color]'\n1694 | \n1695 |         Each of them is optional. If not provided, the value from the style\n1696 |         cycle is used. Exception: If ``line`` is given, but no ``marker``,\n1697 |         the data will be a line without markers.\n1698 | \n1699 |         Other combinations such as ``[color][marker][line]`` are also\n1700 |         supported, but note that their parsing may be ambiguous.\n1701 | \n1702 |         **Markers**\n1703 | \n1704 |         =============   ===============================\n1705 |         character       description\n1706 |         =============   ===============================\n1707 |         ``'.'``         point marker\n1708 |         ``','``         pixel marker\n1709 |         ``'o'``         circle marker\n1710 |         ``'v'``         triangle_down marker\n1711 |         ``'^'``         triangle_up marker\n1712 |         ``'<'``         triangle_left marker\n1713 |         ``'>'``         triangle_right marker\n1714 |         ``'1'``         tri_down marker\n1715 |         ``'2'``         tri_up marker\n1716 |         ``'3'``         tri_left marker\n1717 |         ``'4'``         tri_right marker\n1718 |         ``'8'``         octagon marker\n1719 |         ``'s'``         square marker\n1720 |         ``'p'``         pentagon marker\n1721 |         ``'P'``         plus (filled) marker\n1722 |         ``'*'``         star marker\n1723 |         ``'h'``         hexagon1 marker\n1724 |         ``'H'``         hexagon2 marker\n1725 |         ``'+'``         plus marker\n1726 |         ``'x'``         x marker\n1727 |         ``'X'``         x (filled) marker\n1728 |         ``'D'``         diamond marker\n1729 |         ``'d'``         thin_diamond marker\n1730 |         ``'|'``         vline marker\n1731 |         ``'_'``         hline marker\n1732 |         =============   ===============================\n1733 | \n1734 |         **Line Styles**\n1735 | \n1736 |         =============    ===============================\n1737 |         character        description\n1738 |         =============    ===============================\n1739 |         ``'-'``          solid line style\n1740 |         ``'--'``         dashed line style\n1741 |         ``'-.'``         dash-dot line style\n1742 |         ``':'``          dotted line style\n1743 |         =============    ===============================\n1744 | \n1745 |         Example format strings::\n1746 | \n1747 |             'b'    # blue markers with default shape\n1748 |             'or'   # red circles\n1749 |             '-g'   # green solid line\n1750 |             '--'   # dashed line with default color\n1751 |             '^k:'  # black triangle_up markers connected by a dotted line\n1752 | \n1753 |         **Colors**\n1754 | \n1755 |         The supported color abbreviations are the single letter codes\n1756 | \n1757 |         =============    ===============================\n1758 |         character        color\n1759 |         =============    ===============================\n1760 |         ``'b'``          blue\n1761 |         ``'g'``          green\n1762 |         ``'r'``          red\n1763 |         ``'c'``          cyan\n1764 |         ``'m'``          magenta\n1765 |         ``'y'``          yellow\n1766 |         ``'k'``          black\n1767 |         ``'w'``          white\n1768 |         =============    ===============================\n1769 | \n1770 |         and the ``'CN'`` colors that index into the default property cycle.\n1771 | \n1772 |         If the color is the only part of the format string, you can\n1773 |         additionally use any  `matplotlib.colors` spec, e.g. full names\n1774 |         (``'green'``) or hex strings (``'#008000'``).\n1775 |         \"\"\"\n1776 |         kwargs = cbook.normalize_kwargs(kwargs, mlines.Line2D)\n1777 |         lines = [*self._get_lines(self, *args, data=data, **kwargs)]\n1778 |         for line in lines:\n1779 |             self.add_line(line)\n1780 |         if scalex:\n1781 |             self._request_autoscale_view(\"x\")\n1782 |         if scaley:\n1783 |             self._request_autoscale_view(\"y\")\n1784 |         return lines\n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes._parse_scatter_color_args`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n4539 | def _parse_scatter_color_args(c, edgecolors, kwargs, xsize,\n4540 |                                   get_next_color_func):\n4541 |         \"\"\"\n4542 |         Helper function to process color related arguments of `.Axes.scatter`.\n4543 | \n4544 |         Argument precedence for facecolors:\n4545 | \n4546 |         - c (if not None)\n4547 |         - kwargs['facecolor']\n4548 |         - kwargs['facecolors']\n4549 |         - kwargs['color'] (==kwcolor)\n4550 |         - 'b' if in classic mode else the result of ``get_next_color_func()``\n4551 | \n4552 |         Argument precedence for edgecolors:\n4553 | \n4554 |         - kwargs['edgecolor']\n4555 |         - edgecolors (is an explicit kw argument in scatter())\n4556 |         - kwargs['color'] (==kwcolor)\n4557 |         - 'face' if not in classic mode else None\n4558 | \n4559 |         Parameters\n4560 |         ----------\n4561 |         c : :mpltype:`color` or array-like or list of :mpltype:`color` or None\n4562 |             See argument description of `.Axes.scatter`.\n4563 |         edgecolors : :mpltype:`color` or sequence of color or {'face', 'none'} or None\n4564 |             See argument description of `.Axes.scatter`.\n4565 |         kwargs : dict\n4566 |             Additional kwargs. If these keys exist, we pop and process them:\n4567 |             'facecolors', 'facecolor', 'edgecolor', 'color'\n4568 |             Note: The dict is modified by this function.\n4569 |         xsize : int\n4570 |             The size of the x and y arrays passed to `.Axes.scatter`.\n4571 |         get_next_color_func : callable\n4572 |             A callable that returns a color. This color is used as facecolor\n4573 |             if no other color is provided.\n4574 | \n4575 |             Note, that this is a function rather than a fixed color value to\n4576 |             support conditional evaluation of the next color.  As of the\n4577 |             current implementation obtaining the next color from the\n4578 |             property cycle advances the cycle. This must only happen if we\n4579 |             actually use the color, which will only be decided within this\n4580 |             method.\n4581 | \n4582 |         Returns\n4583 |         -------\n4584 |         c\n4585 |             The input *c* if it was not *None*, else a color derived from the\n4586 |             other inputs or defaults.\n4587 |         colors : array(N, 4) or None\n4588 |             The facecolors as RGBA values, or *None* if a colormap is used.\n4589 |         edgecolors\n4590 |             The edgecolor.\n4591 | \n4592 |         \"\"\"\n4593 |         facecolors = kwargs.pop('facecolors', None)\n4594 |         facecolors = kwargs.pop('facecolor', facecolors)\n4595 |         edgecolors = kwargs.pop('edgecolor', edgecolors)\n4596 | \n4597 |         kwcolor = kwargs.pop('color', None)\n4598 | \n4599 |         if kwcolor is not None and c is not None:\n4600 |             raise ValueError(\"Supply a 'c' argument or a 'color'\"\n4601 |                              \" kwarg but not both; they differ but\"\n4602 |                              \" their functionalities overlap.\")\n4603 | \n4604 |         if kwcolor is not None:\n4605 |             try:\n4606 |                 mcolors.to_rgba_array(kwcolor)\n4607 |             except ValueError as err:\n4608 |                 raise ValueError(\n4609 |                     \"'color' kwarg must be a color or sequence of color \"\n4610 |                     \"specs.  For a sequence of values to be color-mapped, use \"\n4611 |                     \"the 'c' argument instead.\") from err\n4612 |             if edgecolors is None:\n4613 |                 edgecolors = kwcolor\n4614 |             if facecolors is None:\n4615 |                 facecolors = kwcolor\n4616 | \n4617 |         if edgecolors is None and not mpl.rcParams['_internal.classic_mode']:\n4618 |             edgecolors = mpl.rcParams['scatter.edgecolors']\n4619 | \n4620 |         c_was_none = c is None\n4621 |         if c is None:\n4622 |             c = (facecolors if facecolors is not None\n4623 |                  else \"b\" if mpl.rcParams['_internal.classic_mode']\n4624 |                  else get_next_color_func())\n4625 |         c_is_string_or_strings = (\n4626 |             isinstance(c, str)\n4627 |             or (np.iterable(c) and len(c) > 0\n4628 |                 and isinstance(cbook._safe_first_finite(c), str)))\n4629 | \n4630 |         def invalid_shape_exception(csize, xsize):\n4631 |             return ValueError(\n4632 |                 f\"'c' argument has {csize} elements, which is inconsistent \"\n4633 |                 f\"with 'x' and 'y' with size {xsize}.