se a lazy copy mechanism to defer the copy and\n1141 |                 ignore the `copy` keyword. The `copy` keyword will be removed in a\n1142 |                 future version of pandas.\n1143 | \n1144 |                 You can already get the future behavior and improvements through\n1145 |                 enabling copy on write ``pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True``\n1146 | \n1147 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n1148 |         inplace : bool, default False\n1149 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Series\n1150 |             or DataFrame.\n1151 | \n1152 |         Returns\n1153 |         -------\n1154 |         DataFrame, or None\n1155 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n1156 | \n1157 |         See Also\n1158 |         --------\n1159 |         Series.rename : Alter Series index labels or name.\n1160 |         DataFrame.rename : Alter DataFrame index labels or name.\n1161 |         Index.rename : Set new names on index.\n1162 | \n1163 |         Notes\n1164 |         -----\n1165 |         ``DataFrame.rename_axis`` supports two calling conventions\n1166 | \n1167 |         * ``(index=index_mapper, columns=columns_mapper, ...)``\n1168 |         * ``(mapper, axis={'index', 'columns'}, ...)``\n1169 | \n1170 |         The first calling convention will only modify the names of\n1171 |         the index and/or the names of the Index object that is the columns.\n1172 |         In this case, the parameter ``copy`` is ignored.\n1173 | \n1174 |         The second calling convention will modify the names of the\n1175 |         corresponding index if mapper is a list or a scalar.\n1176 |         However, if mapper is dict-like or a function, it will use the\n1177 |         deprecated behavior of modifying the axis *labels*.\n1178 | \n1179 |         We *highly* recommend using keyword arguments to clarify your\n1180 |         intent.\n1181 | \n1182 |         Examples\n1183 |         --------\n1184 |       