     -------\n2280 |         DataFrame\n2281 |             The filtered subset of the original DataFrame.\n2282 | \n2283 |         See Also\n2284 |         --------\n2285 |         DataFrame.filter: Filter elements of ungrouped DataFrame.\n2286 |         SeriesGroupBy.filter : Filter elements from groups base on criterion.\n2287 | \n2288 |         Notes\n2289 |         -----\n2290 |         Each subframe is endowed the attribute 'name' in case you need to know\n2291 |         which group you are working on.\n2292 | \n2293 |         Functions that mutate the passed object can produce unexpected\n2294 |         behavior or errors and are not supported. See :ref:`gotchas.udf-mutation`\n2295 |         for more details.\n2296 | \n2297 |         Examples\n2298 |         --------\n2299 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n2300 |         ...     {\n2301 |         ...         \"A\": [\"foo\", \"bar\", \"foo\", \"bar\", \"foo\", \"bar\"],\n2302 |         ...         \"B\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6],\n2303 |         ...         \"C\": [2.0, 5.0, 8.0, 1.0, 2.0, 9.0],\n2304 |         ...     }\n2305 |         ... )\n2306 |         >>> grouped = df.groupby(\"A\")\n2307 |         >>> grouped.filter(lambda x: x[\"B\"].mean() > 3.0)\n2308 |              A  B    C\n2309 |         1  bar  2  5.0\n2310 |         3  bar  4  1.0\n2311 |         5  bar  6  9.0\n2312 |         \"\"\"\n2313 |         indices = []\n2314 | \n2315 |         obj = self._selected_obj\n2316 |         gen = self._grouper.get_iterator(obj)\n2317 | \n2318 |         for name, group in gen:\n2319 |             # 2023-02-27 no tests are broken this pinning, but it is documented in the\n2320 |             #  docstring above.\n2321 |             object.__setattr__(group, \"name\", name)\n2322 | \n2323 |             res = func(group, *args, **kwargs)\n2324 | \n2325 |             try:\n2326 |                 res = res.squeeze()\n2327 |             except AttributeError:  # allow e.g., scalars and frames to pass\n2328 |                 pass\n2329 | \n2330 |             # interpret the result of the filter\n2331 |             if is_bool(res) or (is_scalar(res) and isna(res)):\n2332 |                 if notna(res) and res:\n2333 |                     indices.append(self._get_index(name))\n2334 |             else:\n2335 |                 # non scalars aren't allowed\n2336 |                 raise TypeError(\n2337 |                     f\"filter function returned a {type(res).__name__}, \"\n2338 |                     \"but expected a scalar bool\"\n2339 |                 )\n2340 | \n2341 |         return self._apply_filter(indices, dropna)\n```\n\nFound file `pandas/core/generic.py`.\nFound file `pandas/core/dtypes/generic.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me check the implementation of the squeeze method to understand its type hints better:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`.\n```\n841 | def squeeze(self, axis: Axis | None = None):\n842 |         \"\"\"\n843 |         Squeeze 1 dimensional axis objects into scalars.\n844 | \n845 |         Series or DataFrames with a single element are squeezed to a scalar.\n846 |         DataFrames with a single column or a single row are squeezed to a\n847 |         Series. Otherwise the object is unchanged.\n848 | \n849 |         This method is most useful when you don't know if your\n850 |         object is a Series or DataFrame, but you do know it has just a single\n851 |         column. In that case you can safely call `squeeze` to ensure you have a\n852 |         Series.\n853 | \n854 |         Parameters\n855 |         ----------\n856 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}, default None\n857 |             A specific axis to squeeze. By default, all length-1 axes are\n858 |             squeezed. For `Series` this parameter is unused and defaults to `None`.\n859 | \n860 |         Returns\n861 |         -------\n862 |         DataFrame, Series, or scalar\n863 |             The projection after squeezing `axis` or all the axes.\n864 | \n865 |         See Also\n866 |         --------\n867 |         Series.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting scalars.\n868 |         DataFrame.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting Series.\n869 |         Series.to_frame : Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 |         >>> df_a\n914 |            a\n915 |         0  1\n916 |         1  3\n917 | \n918 |         So the columns can be squeezed down, resulting in a Series:\n919 | \n920 |         >>> df_a.squeeze(\"columns\")\n921 |         0    1\n922 |         1    3\n923 |         Name: a, dtype: int64\n924 | \n925 |         Slicing a single row from a single column will produce a single\n926 |         scalar DataFrame:\n927 | \n928 |         >>> df_0a = df.loc[df.index < 1, [\"a\"]]\n929 |         >>> df_0a\n930 |            a\n931 |         0  1\n932 | \n933 |         Squeezing the rows produces a single scalar Series:\n934 | \n935 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze(\"rows\")\n936 |         a    1\n937 |         Name: 0, dtype: int64\n938 | \n939 |         Squeezing all axes will project directly into a scalar:\n940 | \n941 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze()\n942 |         1\n943 |         \"\"\"\n944 |         axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)\n945 |         result = self.iloc[\n946 |             tuple(\n947 |                 0 if i in axes and len(a) == 1 else slice(None)\n948 |                 for i, a in enumerate(self.axes)\n949 |             )\n950 |         ]\n951 |         if isinstance(result, NDFrame):\n952 |             result = result.__finalize__(self, method=\"squeeze\")\n953 |         return result\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me explore the dependencies of the squeeze method to understand how it interacts with other components:\n\n<function=explore_tree_structure>\n<parameter=start_entities>['pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze']</parameter>\n<parameter=direction>both</parameter>\n<parameter=traversal_depth>2</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "explo